References: <CAEe0rLRxLHD=SAVQbfyLKff9jYq7HiysQt1TGfhjc8o55DxaMQ@mail.gmail.com> <CANHDHZw1Pcx8Vudu2iiFXtD_sY7v7a_wbPPWexRAH3P=t_Hbmw@mail.gmail.com> <CAEe0rLSX8SVJ_tSF8HK_inGWLHVddwVMYHUy+doho+RepVh-Ew@mail.gmail.com>
Message-ID: <CANHDHZww+ZZL9-NLehweB2W8swbCvp2j7xDJ6qeA8YXCXVRK7A@mail.gmail.com>

Hi Bowy, I would not use propensity scores for matching; see this paper
<http://gking.harvard.edu/publications/why-propensity-scores-should-not-be-used-formatching>.
Also, genetic matching gives you only an approximation to the frontier; you
might be interested in this paper
<http://gking.harvard.edu/publications/balance-sample-size-frontier-matching-methods-causal-inference>
and
software, which computes the frontier exactly, and is also about 1M times
faster. But your point is still relevant: if you have a view as to the
right metric for our data, then you probably want to use that for both
matching and the modeling that would follow.

Gary
